      "cross_epoch_justification": "The in-sample window crosses one epoch boundary: stt_rise_2026 (2026-04-01), the rise in\nsecurities transaction tax on options. It is permitted here, for one reason that is about\nthis specific boundary and not a general waiver:\n\nA tax-rate change alters the COST LEVEL of the trade. It does not alter the population of\ntrades, the contract specification, the expiry calendar, the margin regime or the flow that\nthe hypothesis is about. C5 charges both regimes at their own date-effective rates within\nthe one run, so no session is charged the wrong rate, and the metric this MVP treats as its\nheadline \u2014 the cost-breakeven multiple \u2014 is precisely the number that absorbs a change in\ncost level. Pooling the two sides therefore pools two cost regimes correctly charged, not\ntwo different markets.\n\nThe alternative was to put the holdout boundary on 2026-04-01, which would make both sides\nepoch-clean and cost nothing but sample: 60 in-sample sessions instead of 80, on the side\nwhere the verdict is actually decided. Against a sample already too short, that was the\nworse trade. Recorded so the choice is visible rather than inferred.\n\nThis does NOT justify a window crossing weekly_expiry_cull_2024 or nse_expiry_tuesday_2025.\nThose changed the traded object; this changed a rate. The gate's own docstring notes that\nthis field is per-gate and static, so it is stated narrowly on purpose: this gate grades one\nwindow, and the boundary named above is the only one that window crosses.\n",
